// A circuit written badly on purpose, to show the optimiser working.
//
// Concept
//   Each line here is redundant in a way one of QForge's passes can prove.
//   Compile it twice and compare:
//
//     qforge inspect examples/optimisable.qf --ir
//     qforge inspect examples/optimisable.qf --optimized
//
//   The second prints the pass report explaining every removal.
//
// What each pass finds
//   `h q[0]` twice            -> cancel-self-inverse (H H = I)
//   `s` then `sdg`            -> cancel-inverse-pair (exact inverses)
//   `rx(0.3)` then `rx(-0.3)` -> merge-rotations, then remove-identity
//   `id q[1]`                 -> remove-identity
//   `rz(4 * pi)`              -> remove-identity (a rotation is I at 4*pi)
//   `rz(2 * pi)`              -> KEPT: at 2*pi a rotation is -I, not I
//
//   The barrier is a fence: the two `x q[1]` gates around it are *not*
//   cancelled, because a barrier tells the optimiser not to reorder across it.
//
// Expected quantum behaviour
//   The surviving circuit is `rz(2 * pi) q[0]; x q[1]; barrier; x q[1];`,
//   whose net effect on |00> is to multiply by -1 - a global phase. So the
//   measured distribution is identical to doing nothing at all.
//
// Expected measurement results
//   `c` is 00 in 100% of shots, optimised or not. That equality is the point:
//   the passes must not change what the program computes.
//
// @expect qubits=2 clbits=2 gates=11
// @expect-outcome 00

qubit[2] q;
bit[2] c;

h q[0];
h q[0];

s q[0];
sdg q[0];

rx(0.3) q[0];
rx(-0.3) q[0];

rz(4 * pi) q[0];
rz(2 * pi) q[0];

id q[1];

x q[1];
barrier;
x q[1];

measure q[0] -> c[0];
measure q[1] -> c[1];
